 # Reconstruction and Compositing Techniques
 
-Also called: Volume Compositing, Ray Casting Compositing, Optical Compositing
 
 ## Overview
-Reconstruction and Compositing Techniques constitute the final image-order rendering stage in direct volume ray casting, where discrete volumetric samples are combined into a continuous 2D image [S1, unofficial][S2]. This family solves the problem of projecting a 3D density and color field into a 2D pixel buffer while preserving internal structure, transparency, and lighting interactions, which binary classification methods fail to do due to false positives and negatives [S4, unofficial]. It is distinguished from its siblings by its reliance on iterative accumulation of color and opacity using the OVER operator, enabling optimizations like early ray termination, and its integration with various reconstruction kernels and differentiable training loops [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
+Reconstruction and Compositing Techniques encompass the mathematical and algorithmic methods used to transform discrete volumetric data into continuous signals and combine those signals into final pixel colors. This family includes methods for evaluating polynomial fields along rays, resampling discrete data via convolution, and integrating optical properties using various compositing models. It is distinguished from sibling methods by its focus on the underlying signal processing (reconstruction) and the specific mathematical formulation of the accumulation (compositing), ranging from standard alpha blending to differentiable training passes and importance-driven modulation.
 
